// SYNTAX TEST "source.nota" "doc-state sugar is deliberately UNPAINTED (semantic tokens own <label> / &ref / [^fn])"

// The conservative grammar leaves context-sensitive doc-state sugar unclaimed: <, & and [^ are far
// too easily confused with comparison operators, ampersands and brackets to decide line-locally, so
// the previous grammar's boundary-guarded rules are GONE. The reader-driven semantic tokens paint
// these faithfully; here we only prove the first-paint grammar makes no claim (and no longer emits
// the removed entity.name.label / variable.other.reference / footnote scopes).

A section <sec_intro> a ref &sec_intro a note[^n1].
//        ^ source.nota - entity.name.label.nota
//         ^^^^^^^^^ source.nota - variable.other.interpolation.nota
//                  ^ source.nota - punctuation.definition.label.end.nota
//                          ^ source.nota - variable.other.reference.nota
//                           ^^^^^^^^^ source.nota - variable.other.interpolation.nota
//                                             ^^ source.nota - punctuation.definition.footnote.begin.nota
//                                               ^^ source.nota - variable.other.footnote.nota

// A footnote DEFINITION line is likewise unpainted (no colon-sugar element, no footnote scope).
[^n1]: The footnote body stays plain prose.
// <- source.nota - punctuation.definition.footnote.begin.nota
//   ^ source.nota - keyword.operator.colon-sugar.nota

// The old boundary-guard cases (Vec<T>, R&D) were always meant to stay literal -- they still do.
Vec<T> and R&D stay literal.
// <- source.nota
//  ^ source.nota - punctuation.definition.label.begin.nota
//          ^ source.nota - punctuation.definition.reference.nota
